## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of streaming quantiles and the trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and latency. It also evaluates familiarity with mergeable sketches for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantiles (TDigest/KLL)
- Mergeable data sketches across shards
- Compression parameter and accuracy vs memory trade-offs
- Update and query time complexities

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: TDigest usage
class TDigest { 
  add(x); 
  quantile(p); 
  merge(other); 
}

const digest = new TDigest(100);
for (const latency of batch) {
  digest.add(latency);
}
const p95 = digest.quantile(0.95);
```

## Performance Comparison
- **TDigest**: O(n log k) total time, O(k) memory, O(log k) per-element update
- **Recomputing**: O(n log n) total time, O(n) memory, O(1) per-element (but requires storage)

The streaming approach enables real-time 95th percentile computation with strict memory limits and supports distributed merging across shards.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of sliding-window quantile estimation with bounded memory—a practical problem in latency monitoring where old samples must fade out over time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance using a circular buffer
- Fixed-bin histogram design and bucket mapping
- Error bounds derived from bucket width

## Bucket Mapping
The value range [0, M) is divided into S equal-width buckets. A value x maps to bucket floor(x * S / M), giving each bucket a width of M/S.

## Choosing S
The number of buckets S trades off memory (O(S)) and accuracy. With bucket width M/S, the P95 estimate has error at most M/(2S). For 1% error, choose S ≈ 50*M. In practice, S = 100-1000 works well for typical latency ranges.

## Error Guarantees vs Exact Computation
- **Approximate P95**: The estimated percentile falls within the bucket containing the true P95. Maximum error is half the bucket width: M/(2S).
- **Exact P95**: Requires maintaining all W samples in the window and sorting them, which is O(W log W) per query.
- **Trade-off**: This histogram approach gives O(1) updates and O(S) queries with bounded error, vs O(1) updates and O(W log W) queries for exact computation.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowP95 {
  constructor(M, W, S) {
    this.M = M;
    this.W = W;
    this.S = S;
    this.buffer = new Int32Array(W);
    this.hist = new Int32Array(S);
    this.head = 0;
    this.count = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const b = Math.floor((x * this.S) / this.M);
    this.hist[b]++;

    if (this.count === this.W) {
      const y = this.buffer[this.head];
      const by = Math.floor((y * this.S) / this.M);
      this.hist[by]--;
    } else {
      this.count++;
    }

    this.buffer[this.head] = x;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
  }

  query() {
    const p95 = Math.floor(0.95 * this.count);
    let cum = 0;
    for (let b = 0; b < this.S; b++) {
      cum += this.hist[b];
      if (cum >= p95) return (b * this.M) / this.S;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for non-uniform distributions or dynamic M?
- How does the choice of S affect query latency in a high-throughput system?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a practical data structure solution for maintaining top-K items under batch updates, balancing per-update cost and output cost, and comparing against naive approaches.

## Key Concepts
- **Top-K maintenance** with dynamic count updates
- **Batch amortization** — distributing work across batch items rather than per-event
- **Heap with lazy updates** — using a min-heap for efficient top-K tracking
- **Space-time trade-offs** — comparing near-linear space with output efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopKTracker {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap(); // stores (count, id) pairs
  }

  processBatch(updates) {
    // Phase 1: Apply all deltas to hashmap
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const newCount = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + delta;
      this.counts.set(id, newCount);
    }

    // Phase 2: Reconcile heap with updated counts
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const count = this.counts.get(id);
      if (this.heap.has(id)) {
        this.heap.update(id, count);
      } else if (this.heap.size < this.k) {
        this.heap.insert(id, count);
      } else if (count > this.heap.peekMin().count) {
        this.heap.replaceMin(id, count);
      }
    }
  }

  getTopK() {
    return this.heap.getAllSorted(); // returns K items sorted by count descending
  }
}
```

## Amortized Analysis
Each batch processes B events. Updating the hashmap costs O(B) total. Each heap operation costs O(log K), and at most B such operations occur per batch, yielding O(B log K) amortized time. Since B << N in practice (batch sizes are typically much smaller than total unique keys), this significantly outperforms re-scanning all N items after every batch, which costs O(N log N) per batch.

